export const jss3VariationTopic='Variation (direct, inverse, joint and partial variation; practical applications)';
export const jss3VariationPlan:TutorPlan={goal:'Understand and solve JSS3 variation problems by identifying the correct proportional model and its constant.',why:'Variation formalises how quantities change together. The key is not a formula list but recognising what remains constant.',outcomes:['Model direct variation y=kx.','Model inverse variation y=k/x.','Model joint variation such as y=kxz.','Model simple partial variation such as y=a+bx from stated conditions.','Solve practical problems and distinguish variation types.'],examFocus:['Translate “varies directly/inversely/jointly” before substituting.','Find the constant from known data first.','For inverse variation preserve the product.','For joint variation include every stated varying factor.','For partial variation use enough conditions to determine the constants.'],units:[
{title:'Meaning of variation and the constant k',terms:[['variation','relationship describing how one quantity changes with another'],['constant of variation','fixed number k linking the quantities']],explain:'A variation statement becomes an equation by introducing k. The known pair(s) determine k; only then should the unknown case be solved.',example:'y varies directly as x. If y=15 when x=3,15=3k so k=5 and y=5x.',check:'If y∝x and y=24 when x=6, find k.',commonMistakes:['omit k','use second data before establishing relation','assume every relation is direct']},
{title:'Direct variation',terms:[['direct variation','y∝x, hence y=kx']],explain:'If x is multiplied by a factor, y is multiplied by the same factor. The ratio y/x remains k.',example:'y=28 when x=7→k=4. When x=12,y=48.',check:'y∝x; y=18 at x=6. Find y at x=15.',commonMistakes:['use xy constant','add differences','reverse ratio']},
{title:'Inverse variation',terms:[['inverse variation','y∝1/x, hence y=k/x']],explain:'The product xy remains k. If x doubles, y halves under the model.',example:'y=12 when x=5→k=60. At x=8,y=7.5.',check:'y∝1/x; y=9 when x=4. Find y when x=6.',commonMistakes:['direct scaling','k=y/x','forget reciprocal']},
{title:'Joint variation',terms:[['joint variation','one quantity varies directly as the product of two or more quantities']],explain:'If y varies jointly as x and z, write y=kxz. All named factors belong in the product.',example:'y=24 when x=3,z=4→k=2. If x=5,z=6,y=60.',check:'p varies jointly as q and r; p=30 when q=5,r=3. Find p when q=4,r=7.',commonMistakes:['use x+z','omit one factor','treat one factor inverse without wording']},
{title:'Combined direct and inverse variation',terms:[['combined variation','direct in one factor and inverse in another']],explain:'A statement may say y varies directly as x and inversely as z, giving y=kx/z. Read every relationship word.',example:'y=10 when x=6,z=3→k=5. At x=8,z=4,y=10.',check:'w∝p/q; w=12,p=8,q=2. Find w when p=15,q=5.',commonMistakes:['multiply denominator factor','ignore inverse wording','wrong k']},
{title:'Partial variation',terms:[['partial variation','a quantity has a fixed part plus a part that varies; simple form y=a+bx']],explain:'In simple JSS3 partial variation, one part is constant and another changes directly with x. Two data pairs can determine a and b by simultaneous equations.',example:'y=a+bx; y=11 at x=2 and y=20 at x=5. Then a+2b=11,a+5b=20→3b=9→b=3,a=5, so y=5+3x.',check:'y=a+bx; y=10 at x=1 and16 at x=3. Find a,b.',commonMistakes:['force y=kx through origin','need two conditions but use one','confuse a with kx']},
{title:'Practical variation models',terms:[['model assumption','condition under which a proportional relationship is valid']],explain:'Fixed-price cost varies directly with quantity; time for fixed work varies inversely with equal workers; some physical/geometry quantities may vary jointly. State the fixed conditions rather than applying variation blindly.',example:'If8 workers finish fixed work in15 days, worker-days=120. Ten equal workers need12 days.',check:'Give one direct and one inverse practical example and state what is fixed.',commonMistakes:['real life always exactly proportional','no units','mix direct/inverse']},
{title:'Building a direct-variation table',terms:[['variation table','table showing corresponding values that obey one variation law']],outcomes:['build a value table from y=kx','recognise the constant ratio y/x'],explain:'A table makes direct variation visible. First determine k. Then calculate each y-value from the same equation. Do not find each row by an unrelated trick. If y=3x, every row must satisfy y/x=3.',example:'For y=3x:\n\n   x | 1 | 2 | 4 | 7\n  ---+---+---+---+---\n   y | 3 | 6 |12 |21\n\nCheck each column: 3/1=6/2=12/4=21/7=3. That unchanged ratio is the constant k.',check:'Complete the table for y=5x when x=2,3,6,10.',commonMistakes:['change the multiplier from one row to another','add a fixed amount instead of using the variation equation','forget to check the constant ratio']},
{title:'Direct variation from a word problem',terms:[['unit rate','amount corresponding to one unit when the relationship is directly proportional']],outcomes:['translate a practical statement into y=kx','keep units throughout the solution'],explain:'Suppose identical exercise books cost the same amount each. Total cost C varies directly with number n, so C=kn, where k is the cost of one book. Use the known purchase to find k before answering a new purchase.',example:'6 books cost ₦2,700.\nC=kn\n2700=6k\nk=2700÷6=450\nSo C=450n.\nFor 14 books:\nC=450×14\n  450\n×  14\n-----\n 1800\n4500\n-----\n6300\nTherefore 14 books cost ₦6,300.',check:'If 8 identical pens cost ₦1,600, find the cost of 15 pens using a variation equation.',commonMistakes:['use the total cost as k','drop the naira unit','scale by addition instead of multiplication']},
{title:'Inverse variation from fixed work',terms:[['worker-days','workers multiplied by days when equal workers complete a fixed amount of work']],outcomes:['form xy=k for inverse variation','explain why increasing one quantity decreases the other'],explain:'For a fixed job and equally productive workers, number of workers w and time d vary inversely: d=k/w, so wd=k. The product represents the same total worker-days.',example:'8 workers take 15 days.\nwd=k\n8×15=120\nSo k=120 worker-days.\nFor 12 workers:\nd=120/12=10 days.\nThe answer must be smaller than 15 days because more equal workers are doing the same fixed job.',check:'6 workers complete a fixed job in 20 days. How long should 15 equal workers take?',commonMistakes:['make time increase with workers','use w/d as the constant','ignore the condition that the amount of work is fixed']},
{title:'Solving partial variation carefully',terms:[['fixed component','part represented by a constant a'],['variable component','part represented by bx']],outcomes:['form two simultaneous equations','solve for both constants','use the resulting model'],explain:'For y=a+bx, one observation cannot determine both a and b. Use two data pairs to create two equations. Subtracting the equations eliminates a and reveals b; then substitute back for a.',example:'y=11 when x=2 and y=20 when x=5.\n\n(1) a+2b=11\n(2) a+5b=20\n\nSubtract (1) from (2):\n    a+5b = 20\n -( a+2b = 11 )\n -------------\n       3b =  9\n        b =  3\n\nUse (1): a+2(3)=11, so a=5.\nTherefore y=5+3x.\nCheck x=5: y=5+15=20 ✓',check:'For y=a+bx, y=13 when x=2 and y=25 when x=6. Find a and b.',commonMistakes:['try to determine two constants from one condition','subtract corresponding terms incorrectly','stop after finding b without finding a']},
{title:'Final variation workflow',terms:[['variation workflow','words→symbolic relation→k→new case→check']],explain:'Translate the statement; insert k; use known values to find k/constants; write the complete equation; substitute the required case; solve and check how the answer should change qualitatively.',example:'“P varies jointly as m and n”→P=kmn, not P=k(m+n).',check:'State the workflow for a joint-variation problem.',commonMistakes:['numbers before model','lost k','no reasonableness check']}
]};
